int4 3D scratch: split the sequence policy from the token allocation

The three softmax_segm_* scratch buffers are indexed by QUERY TOKEN but were
sized by seq_threshold_3D, a SEQUENCE policy. For q=1 decode the units coincide,
which is why this never bit. With DFlash2 multi-query verify they do not.

Worse, capture-enabled mode snaps that policy threshold to the nearest decode
capture size, which on this config SHRINKS it 32 -> 8. So every multi-query batch
of 9..16 tokens silently took the 2D path -- in capture-enabled mode only, i.e.
the only mode that ships. Measured on an RTX 3090 (sm86), Qwen3.8-27B W4A16:
  capture-enabled  q=9 -> 2D, q_token_capacity_failed
  fully eager      q=9 -> 3D
Same config, same request. Forcings in both directions confirmed capacity, not
mode, is the deciding predicate: holding mode fixed and moving only the scratch
rows flips the result each way.

This splits the units and derives the capacity from the engine config objects:
  seq_threshold_3D          SEQUENCES, policy (unchanged; the capture-snap
                            question is deliberately a separate change)
  MAX_QUERY_LEN_3D          QUERY TOKENS PER SEQUENCE; one source for both
                            eligibility and allocation
  scratch_token_capacity_3d TOTAL QUERY TOKENS, allocation:
    min(max_num_batched_tokens,
        min(max_num_seqs, seq_threshold_3D) * MAX_QUERY_LEN_3D)

Cost here: 16 rows, 28.22 MiB across 4 live allocations (+14.11 over stock).
Allocation is per attention-backend instance, NOT per layer (64 layers, 4
allocations), so no shared workspace is needed at this multiplicity. NOTE: the
formula scales with max_num_seqs -- at max_num_seqs=32 it is 903 MiB, which
needs its own performance/VRAM ruling rather than a silent cap. A silent cap
would recreate exactly the accidental fallback this patch removes.

A capacity failure on policy-eligible work marks the backend DEGRADED, increments
an observable counter (mq3d_breach_state()), and emits ONE rate-limited diagnostic
carrying the first specimen. Sixteen identical error lines per request are not
loud, they are floodable, and a flood is how an operator learns to filter the
message out; the state is the signal, not the log volume. 2D remains the safe
fallback throughout. The boot record binds the
capacity, its inputs and its byte cost. Construction asserts all three buffers
carry the declared capacity, so an oversized buffer cannot mask an undersized
sibling.

